d cost management  perspective  next i'll cover our approach to cost  visibility  and share a number of examples of how we  were able to implement various cost  optimizations  we'll finish up with a review of lessons  learned  so the challenge was how to migrate 15  million lines of code  from six data centers worldwide in six  months  openx consumes a significant amount of  compute network  and storage resources and we process  trillions of events per day  in terms of migrating to gcp the  engineering teams did all of the heavy  lifting  and did a fantastic job and the  migration was a success  but early on in the migration effort we  needed to organize how we were going to  get started  so let's talk about that  at the beginning of the project paul  ryan rcto  laid out a high-level cloud migration  roadmap  we started with a focus on governance  whereby we set out to establish controls  around product ownership cost  responsibility  roles and budgets and resource  management  including folder organization project  naming conventions  and required project labels and skew  labels  from that foundation we could focus on  cost visibility  and ultimately on cost optimization  let's first talk about cost visibility  our cost visibility efforts have helped  us understand  what we are spending money on sliced on  a variety of dimensions  for example by project by service  by region and by label to name just a  few dimensions  in addition to aggregate cost visibility  we were focusing on per unit  cost metrics also  we prioritize cost visibility around  usage type  namely preemptable on-demand and  committed use  and we turned on gke usage metering to  measure our kubernetes costs by app  by cluster and region so how do we set  up our cost reporting framework  for some gcp customers the gcp console  may provide a good start to cost  reporting  early on we turned on the billing data  export  which is highly recommended and started  to integrate various data sources  into our recording framework we use data  studio  and bigquery for the majority of our  cost reporting  one of our key metrics is how many ad  requests  do we receive each day and what does it  cost us to process each one  we did this by integrating our gcp  billing data  with our openx performance data this  enabled us to get insights into per unit  costs  as well as gross margin performance  in terms of frequency of reporting we  look at cost reporting daily  and we send out a cost summary email  each day to various teams  now that i've touched on how we set  things up  from a governance cost visibility and  reporting standpoint  we will turn our attention to cost  optimization  this grid represents various cost  optimizations in terms of complexity of  the work  relative to the expected cost saving  benefits  also we have organized the optimizations  into four buckets  usage optimization pricing efficiency  waste reduction and refactoring  we are working on or have completed work  on all of the above  overall we have completed more than 30  significant cloud cost  optimizations i'll share some examples  in the following slides  first i'll share two examples of usage  optimizations  the first usage optimization example is  around saving money on egress  we saw that we were spending a lot of  money on egress between zones  which is called inter zone egress in our  regional clusters  see the orange bars on this chart by  shifting to multi-zonal clusters  we were able to reduce enter zone egress  costs to zero  without impacting other egress costs  the second usage optimization example is  around changing the machine shape  to better match utilization of cpu and  ram  by evaluating resource utilization  within a specific project  we found that we were fully utilizing  memory but under utilizing cpu  see the purple bars by changing the  machine shape  we were able to reduce costs by 25  within this project  the last two examples were about usage  optimization  in the next few examples we'll cover  pricing efficiency  finding the best price solution for your  work  the first pricing efficiency example and  the most important  is around leveraging discounted compute  rates for preemptable machines  and implementing committed use discounts  the red line represents on-demand  compute vcpus  you can see a nice drop-off in our  on-demand usage over time  running more services on preemptable  machines the gold line  had a very dramatic cost reduction  also note that some refactoring may be  required to run preemptable  the blue line represents our increased  three-year commitment  this change also led to a significant  cost reduction  the combination of preemptable machines  and committed use discounts  dramatically reduced our costs  the second pricing efficiency example is  around flat rate pricing for bigquery  we are a heavy user of bigquery both for  storage  and for query we found that our  on-demand analysis costs were very spiky  and we had days where we spent way more  than expected  by committing to flat rate pricing we  were able to better control our costs  the downside is that some queries may  take longer to run  if capacity demands exceeded committed  capacity  in our case our queries were primarily  for analysis purposes  so we were fine making a performance  trade-off  as a result our on-demand cost dropped  and we attained a more stable cost for  bigquery  the third and final prices pricing  efficiency example  is around reducing storage costs by  choosing the most cost-effective storage  class  we reduce storage costs by 50  by shifting from regional storage the  blue bars  to a blend of cold line and near line  storage  also while we are on the subject of  storage  we've done a lot of work with data  retention policy changes  a form of waste reduction and these have  also had a big cost saving  impact so what kind of cost impact did  these and other optimizations have  i mentioned earlier that one of our key  areas of focus was on optimizing our per  unit costs  one important cost metric that we track  is cost per ad request  this chart shows our cost per ad request  over time  through a combination of usage  optimization  pricing efficiency waste reduction  and a fair amount of refactoring we have  reduced our per unit costs by more than  60  since september of last year this is all  during a time of increased ad unit  growth  now we'll wrap up with some lessons  learned  here are some key takeaways from our  experience optimizing costs in gcp  cost management and optimization is not  a one-person job  your success will depend on good  teamwork across the organization  it might be boring work but an early  focus on  governance resource management and  controls  will help pave the way to success in the  cloud  early on we had a few days of pain with  costs that spiraled out of control  at the top of our do-overs list would be  to implement project budgeting  and or cross-spike detection even  earlier than we did  where possible utilize preemptable  machines  and for long term consistent usage that  would otherwise be on demand  it may be worth it to make a one year or  three year commitment  finally there are many different ways to  optimize costs in the cloud  cost optimizations vary with level of  effort and the cost saving impact  so prioritize accordingly now i'll hand  it back to pathetique  thank you  thanks scott for taking us through that  transformative journey of openx  it doesn't matter if you are a startup  or a hundred year old enterprise  process and optimization tips shared in  this session  and by scott can be leveraged across all  types of customers  if you still need help engage google  cloud professional services  to jumpstart your cost performance  journey
 

